From b565fb9675618717a9ea8c79a668d27019e7d83f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AshFall UI <ui@ashfall.local>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 19:21:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Settings: add charging control to the battery page

AshFall #140 v1: the manual charging-separation toggle on
Settings > Battery > Charging control, as the second sequential Settings
patch on the battery page (after #137). One row sits in the PlayTime
category, after the PlayTime row, and opens a sub-screen with the toggle,
the firmware support state and the 80 %/10 % battery-care line.

State lives in persist.ashfall.batt.charge_sep (1 | 0). The switch
applies it immediately, refuses to enable below 20 % or while the charger
is disconnected, and Settings re-applies it from a direct-boot-aware
receiver on LOCKED_BOOT_COMPLETED (BOOT_COMPLETED stays as the fallback),
so separation starts before the first unlock. The setting survives an
unplug: ACTION_POWER_DISCONNECTED is not an implicit-broadcast exemption,
so there is no unplug receiver and no auto-off promise. Instead the boot
path and the page on resume drop a stored request below 20 % and restore
charging, so a stale setting cannot strand a low battery.

The backend is a raw HwBinder client for the retail vendor factory HAL,
vendor.qti.hardware.factory@1.1::IFactory, falling back to @1.0: the RP5
registers 1.1 only (docs/stock/vintf/manifest.xml), and HIDL lookup is an
exact fqname match. The vendor Java stubs are not installed on the RP5
(pm list libraries has no factory jar, and nothing links one into
Settings' classloader), so the previous reflective Class.forName path and
its best-effort uses-library / optional_uses_libs entries are removed:
they never resolved. android.os.HwBinder, HwParcel and IHwBinder are
platform APIs under platform_apis, so the client writes the interface
token and the boolean and transacts code 4, exactly as the disassembled
BpHwFactory::_hidl_chargerEnable does.

The vendor service is disabled and oneshot in init, so the first lookup is
what triggers hwservicemanager's ctl.interface_start; each interface is
looked up 5 times, 300 ms apart, before it counts as absent. Lookups and
the transaction run on a dedicated background thread with the result
posted to the main thread, and the boot receiver holds goAsync() until the
answer arrives, so neither the page nor Settings' main thread waits on the
lazy start. Support is claimed only after a successful transaction:
"Not supported on this device" when both descriptors miss, "Not available
right now" for a resolved service whose call failed, and "Checking..." in
between. The method is void, so no return value is read; the reply's status
word is verified when present and an empty reply is not an error.

A missing service, a proxy that returns null, a RemoteException and a
rejected status all fail open: the property is cleared and charging
restored, a rejected property write is detected by reading it back, and
the boot path does nothing when the stored state is off, so a missing HAL
cannot cause a boot loop. Clearing and restoring report separate messages,
so a failed state clear never claims charging could not be stopped.

The row key is ashfall_charging_control: the page already has a Lineage
row keyed charging_control, which would collide in PreferenceGroup.

Copy follows #155: one short sentence per summary, no property names,
sysfs paths or HAL jargon in user text, and the single real warning
(fuel-gauge drift on long separation sessions) kept as two lines.

No QS tile, default off, no dependency on ashfall-hwd.
